    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with **Josh Naylor** (75.57) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92) headlining our offensive attack, both benefiting immensely from plus-matchups against struggling arms. **Naylor** benefits from a high-upside tilt against Imai, where the SP skill projection boosts his value significantly. Similarly, **Vinnie Pasquantino** is locked into the three-hole against Fedde, with a dynamic platoon edge and favorable SP metrics elevating his efficiency score well into the elite tier. **Tyler Soderstrom** also makes a compelling case for top-play status; his 72.71 score is heavily supported by a 10% park factor bump and a prime batting order position, cementing him as a must-start in this favorable environment.\n\nEfficiency remains the priority, leading to a difficult benching decision for **Trevor Story**. With a score of 46.48, **Trevor Story** narrowly missed the final cut, largely suppressed by a grueling matchup against the elite Wheeler. While **Trevor Story** offers potential, the optimizer flagged his efficiency loss against a tough arm and an elite defensive catcher as prohibitive when compared to the higher-floor options currently in our starting nine. Elsewhere, we are closely monitoring the bottom end of our lineup; **Brice Turang** and **Cole Young** are operating at lower efficiency levels today\u2014the former hampered by the opposing arm\u2019s skill and the latter buried in the nine-hole\u2014but we are prioritizing their potential for stolen bases, our scarcest category, as we lean into our team identity.\n\nWe are proceeding with caution regarding **Mickey Moniak**, whose score of 54.25 is weighted down by a daunting matchup against Skenes. While the platoon advantage is favorable, the sheer skill of the opposing pitcher makes this a volatile start. Additionally, **Corbin Carroll** presents a slight risk today; while he remains in the lineup due to his elite speed profile, his score is softened by a tough look against Gore and an opposing catcher with a high-end pop time. We are maintaining a strict adherence to our 40.0 floor; **Zach McKinstry** failed to clear this threshold and remains safely on the bench to preserve our game caps for more opportunistic windows.",
